31.2.m:
With 'Thou art the strainer of the Vasu, having a hundred streams; thou art the strainer of the Vasus, having a thousand streams',
the strainer is the share of the Vasus;
he makes it for them.
Prajāpati created offspring.
His two cooking pots collapsed.
He saw these two cooking pots;
with them he took [it].
When these two cooking pots are [used],
he puts back the two cooking pots of Prajāpati.
With 'Free from consumption, I unite you with offspring',
he makes them free from consumption.
With 'Becoming abundant through increase of wealth',
he makes them attain abundance.
With 'Swelling, full of honey, full of ghee',
he makes the oblation full of honey, full of ghee for the gods.
With 'Living, may we sit near you living',
he utters a blessing.
With 'Thou art the heat (gharma) of Mātariśvan',
the heat of Mātariśvan is the mid-space.
With 'Thou art heaven, thou art earth',
he made it heaven and earth.
With 'All-sustaining by the highest abode, thou art uncrooked; do not be crooked',
he firms it.
He covers [it] with the strainer;
he unites the milk of the plants and of the beasts.
With 'She is all-life, she is all-embracing, she is all-sustaining',
that [heaven] is all-life, the mid-space is all-embracing, this [earth] is all-sustaining;
he causes these worlds to yield in proper order;
these worlds, yielded, milk for him in proper order.
He consecrates three with a yajus;
three are these worlds;
he places sap in these worlds;
therefore creatures live upon these worlds;
he becomes one upon whom others live, who knows thus.
With 'Offered is the drop, offered is the droplet', he consecrates [it],
against spilling.
With 'To Agni, the high firmament, svāhā',
this [earth] is Agni, the high firmament;
much of this [milk] spills while being milked;
he establishes it on this [earth],
against spilling.
He should not milk into a wooden vessel;
a wooden vessel contains fire;
he would sacrifice with stale oblation.
And as to that, the Dārteyas used to say: 'The cake is the mouth of the oblation; not from this is there the staleness of the milk-oblation; it should indeed be milked.'
A Śūdra should not milk;
he is born from the non-existent;
it would be non-existent.
Whatever indeed passes beyond the strainer, that is oblation.
A Śūdra should not milk the Agnihotra;
for they do not purify that.
With 'Mix yourselves, O righteous ones', he pours back,
for cookedness;
for the gods desire what is cooked.
With 'Thee, Indra's share, I curdle with Soma',
he makes it Soma;
Soma indeed is the mystic sānnāyya of the gods;
his Soma-drinking is continuous, who, knowing thus, sacrifices with the sānnāyya.
With 'Thou art unconquered for Viṣṇu',
Viṣṇu is the sacrifice;
he makes it unconquered for the sacrifice.
He should not cover [it] with an earthen vessel;
if he were to cover it with an earthen vessel, the oblation would have the ancestors as its deity.
He covers it with a wooden vessel;
a wooden vessel contains fire;
Agni guards the oblation.
He should make it watery;
water smites demons;
[it is] for the smiting of the demons.
With 'O Viṣṇu, guard the oblation',
Viṣṇu guards the oblation.
With 'O waters, be awake',
the waters are the guardians of the sacrifice;
he commits it to them.

31.7.m:
With 'At the impulse of the god Savitṛ',
impelled by Savitṛ he pours it together for the deities.
With 'Pleasing to such-and-such',
for whichever deity he pours it together, he makes it the oblation;
he also makes it pleasing to that [deity].
He pours it together over the purifier;
he makes it the oblation.
He pours the waters on.
The waters are ambrosia;
he makes the oblation ambrosia.
With 'United are the waters with the plants, united the plants with the sap',
these plants invigorate one another.
With 'What of you is rich, [belonging] to the rich; what of you is fit for oblation, [belonging] to that fit for oblation; what of you is living, [belonging] to the living',
the waters are rich, the plants full of honey, cattle are living;
having united the waters, plants, and cattle into one for him, he makes them full of honey.
With 'For thee that generates', he mixes [it];
he creates a pair.
With 'Thou art the head of Makha',
Makha is the sacrifice;
he makes the head of the sacrifice.
With 'Thou art the cauldron, all life, spread wide, may the lord of the sacrifice spread wide',
he spreads it.
With 'Burnt up is the demon',
he smites away the demons from the mouth of the sacrifice.
This cauldron is indeed set on the fire every half-month, namely the puroḍāśa;
being unappeased, it would be capable of burning up the cattle of the sacrificer.
When he carries the fire around it,
he makes it an animal,
for appeasement, to prevent burning up.
He carries [it] around thrice;
three are these worlds;
from these worlds he appeases it.
When the puroḍāśa was placed on the fire, the demons wished to kill it;
Agni, the demon-killer named Naka, was in heaven;
he smote away the demons from it.
With 'May the god Savitṛ cook thee upon the highest firmament',
[it is] for the smiting away of the demons.
With 'May Agni not injure thy body',
[it is] for non-injury;
he causes it to take a crust.
Therefore the brain is covered around.
The potsherds that belong to the head are these potsherds;
the puroḍāśa is the brain.
If he should not cover it over, the brain would be exposed;
when he covers it over,
therefore the brain is hidden.
He covers it over with ashes;
therefore the bone is covered with flesh.
He covers it over with the veda;
therefore the head is covered with hair.
The gods did not find him on whom they could wipe off the cruel [part] of the sacrifice.
Agni said: 'I will generate for you him on whom ye will wipe off the cruel [part] of the sacrifice.'
He cast [a drop] with a burning coal onto the waters;
thence Ekata was born;
a second;
thence Dvita;
a third;
thence Trita.
That he fashioned [them] from himself, that is the selfhood of those belonging to the self;
that he fashioned [them] from the waters, that is the water-nature of those belonging to water.
He pours [it] out inside the vedi;
he obtains that very [blessing].
He sprinkles it with a burning brand,
for cookedness;
for the gods desire what is cooked.
They went, wiping [the guilt] off one after another;
they wiped it off on the one upon whom the sun rises [asleep];
the one upon whom the sun rises, on the one upon whom the sun sets [asleep];
the one upon whom the sun sets, on the one with deformed nails;
the one with deformed nails, on the one with black teeth;
the one with black teeth, on the one whose younger brother married first;
the one whose younger brother married first, on the younger brother who married before his elder;
the younger brother who married before his elder, on the husband of an elder sister whose younger is married;
the husband of an elder sister whose younger is married, on the slayer of a man;
the slayer of a man, on the slayer of a brahmin;
the slayer of a brahmin, on the slayer of an embryo;
guilt does not pass beyond the slayer of an embryo.

31.8.m:
With 'At the impulse of the god Savitṛ', he takes up the wooden sword [sphya];
impelled by Savitṛ he takes it up along with the deities.
With 'Thou art Indra's right arm, thousand-spiked, hundred-edged',
the wooden sword is indeed a thunderbolt;
he sharpens the thunderbolt itself,
being about to strike his rival. With 'O earth, divine place of sacrifice, let me not injure the root of thy plant',
[it is] for the non-injury of the plants.
With 'Go to the pen, the cow-stall',
the metres are indeed the pen, the cow-stall;
he makes the metres a pen, a cow-stall for him.
With 'May thy heaven rain',
he brings down rain itself.
With 'Bind, O god Savitṛ, in the furthest earth with a hundred fetters',
a man has two rivals: he whom he hates, and he who hates him;
both of these he binds for him in the furthest earth with a hundred fetters.
With 'May not thy drop leap to heaven',
the drop is whatever sap she has;
these creatures live upon it;
he restrains it upon her,
so that it should not leap away.
With 'Let not Araru fly to heaven',
Araru is indeed the rival;
he thrusts the rival away from the heavenly world.
There was an Asura named Araru;
he feared, 'The gods will overcome us by the sacrifice.'
He smeared the earth with poison, making it impure.
Indra slew Vṛtra;
his blood ran along this [earth];
it became impure.
When he digs up,
whatever of it is impure, unsuited for sacrifice, that he smites away.
There was an Asura named Araru;
he feared, 'The gods will thrust me out from the earth.'
He lay creeping along the earth.
Indra noticed him.
With 'Drive away Araru, the non-sacrificial place, from the earth, the sacrificial place', he smote him from the earth.
He flew to heaven.
With 'Let not Araru fly to heaven', he thrust him back from heaven.
Then the gods prospered, the Asuras were vanquished.
For whomsoever knowing thus the stambayajus is carried, and who, knowing thus, carries it, he excludes the rival from these worlds; he prospers in himself, his rival is vanquished.
This earth belonged to the Asuras;
the gods said, 'Give us of this.'
They said, 'Speak for yourselves.'
Agni conquered the eastern quarter;
the Vasus the southern, the Rudras the western, the Ādityas the northern.
The gods found this [earth] from the Asuras.
Then the gods prospered, the Asuras were vanquished.
He who, knowing thus, encloses the altar with these deities, wins this [earth] of his rival; he prospers in himself, his rival is vanquished.
What was pure and what was impure went apart from the earth;
the pure went eastward and northward;
the impure westward and southward.
He should make the altar sloping eastward and northward;
he makes it pure, fit for sacrifice;
and also for the separation of the pure and the impure.
He raises the two arms eastward;
he encloses the Āhavanīya fire.
The hips [of the altar] are westward;
he encloses the Gārhapatya fire.
The demons indeed crawl along the root;
he should not cut it with the fingernail.
If he should cut it with the fingernail, he would become one with deformed nails;
he cuts it with the wooden sword.
The wooden sword is indeed a thunderbolt;
with the thunderbolt he slays the demons.

31.9.m:
Prajāpati was both ghee and honey.
From where the honey was, from there he created creatures;
therefore, there is as it were generation from honey;
therefore, they do not perform rites with honey,
for that has lost its freshness.
They perform rites with ghee;
for this sacrificial butter [ājya] belonging to Prajāpati has not lost its freshness;
fresh among the gods is Prajāpati.
He sets it on the Gārhapatya fire;
the wife looks at it;
this is indeed the wife's taking hold of the sacrifice;
and he places a pair at the opening of the sacrifice
for generation.
What the wife does at the sacrifice, that is a pair.
In that the wife looks at it,
he makes a pair.
That which the wife looks at is impure, unsuited for sacrifice;
he sets it on the Āhavanīya fire;
he makes it pure, fit for sacrifice.
The gods and the Asuras did indeed perform equally at the sacrifice;
whatever the gods did, that the Asuras did.
Indra saw this glance [avakāśa];
with it he looked at it;
with it he overcame the Asuras.
Then the gods prospered, the Asuras were vanquished.
He who, knowing thus, looks at the sacrificial butter with this, overcomes his rival;
he prospers in himself, his rival is vanquished.
With 'Burnt up is the demon',
he smites away the demons from the opening of the sacrifice.
With 'Ye are unwhetted, destroying the rival',
he makes them unwhetted.
He cleanses the ladles;
he purifies them.
He cleanses the dipping-spoon [sruva] first;
he sharpens their male first;
then the Juhū,
then the Upabhṛt,
then the Dhruvā.
Yonder [heaven] is the Juhū, the intermediate space the Upabhṛt, this [earth] the Dhruvā;
he cleanses these worlds in order of precedence.
The Juhū belongs to the deity of the sacrificer, the Upabhṛt to the deity of the rival.
Scooping four times into the Juhū, he takes more sacrificial butter;
scooping eight times into the Upabhṛt, less;
he makes his rival an underling to him.

33.2.m:
"The Atirātra is the highest of sacrificial rites," the theologians say; "why do they undertake it first?"
In that they undertake the Atirātra, they undertake the Agniṣṭoma first, then the Ukthya, then the Ṣoḍaśin, then the Atirātra.
Having undertaken the sacrificial rites according to precedence, having taken hold of them, having encompassed them, they sit drinking soma.
They undertake the Jyotiṣṭoma first.
The Jyotiṣṭoma is indeed the mouth of the stomas;
from the mouth itself they thus employ the stomas.
The Jyotiṣṭoma attains to the virāj: the Go is redundant by one, the Āyus is deficient by one;
being praised together, they attain to the virāj.
Two verses remain over.
The virāj is the heavenly world;
the sacrificers are two-footed;
in the heavenly world, in the virāj, the two-footed sacrificers become established.
The Rathantara is the pṛṣṭha.
This earth is the Rathantara;
having taken hold of this very [earth], they sit.
"The Rathantara is by day, the Rathantara is by night," the theologians say; "how is that not monotonous?"
The Saubhara is the Brahma-sāman at the third pressing; it is great [Bṛhat].
He places it in the middle,
for the separation of the Rathantara;
thereby it is not monotonous.
From the Atirātra they undertake the twenty-fourth day.
Twenty-four are the half-months of the year;
the year is reached half-month by half-month;
half-month by half-month they thus reach the year.
Its stotriya verses are three hundred and sixty;
three hundred and sixty indeed are the days of the year;
the year is reached day by day;
day by day they thus reach the year.
It is an Ukthya;
ukthas are cattle;
they win cattle.
The Bṛhat is the pṛṣṭha;
the heavenly world is the Bṛhat;
they reach the heavenly world by it.
The sāman called Trayastriṃśa is at the midday Pavamāna;
thirty-three are the gods;
thereby they reach the gods.
For this indeed they undertake the year;
they reached the gods,
they reached the heavenly world,
they won cattle;
they reached the year half-month by half-month,
they reached [it] day by day.
On that twenty-fourth day, having encompassed all the deities, they sit drinking soma.
Those who undertake the year turned away from here do not attain it safely;
and those who undertake it turned hither from over there, they cross over it safely.
In that [they do] thus, they undertake it turned hither from over there.

33.3.m:
They undertake the Jyotiṣṭoma first;
by it they become established in this world.
They undertake the Goṣṭoma second;
by it they become established in the intermediate space.
They undertake the Āyuṣṭoma last;
by it they become established in that world.
This world is indeed light, the intermediate space is the cow, and yonder [world] is life.
In that they undertake these stomas,
the sacrificial session performers go establishing themselves in these worlds.
The Jyotiṣṭoma attains to the virāj: the Go is redundant by one, the Āyus is deficient by one.
When chanted, they attain to the virāj.
The virāj is vigor.
In that they go by these stomas,
the session performers go securing vigor.
They do not incur the affliction of hunger;
they do not become hungry,
for the session performers are, as it were, pressed hard by hunger.
The Agniṣṭomas are on both sides;
they are the rims.
The Ukthyas are in the middle;
they are the navel.
This is the moving chariot-wheel of the gods.
In that they go by this six-day period,
they mount the chariot-wheel of the gods
for safety.
They attain well-being.
They go with light on both sides.
The heavenly world is indeed light;
the session performers thus go establishing themselves on both sides in the heavenly world.
They go by a six-day period;
the seasons are indeed six;
they go establishing themselves in the seasons.
There are two six-day periods;
they amount to twelve days.
Ten indeed are the vital breaths in man, the breasts are eleventh and twelfth;
thus they turn around man.
There are three six-day periods;
they amount to eighteen days:
nine and nine;
nine are the vital breaths;
thus they turn around the vital breaths.
There are four six-day periods;
they amount to twenty-four days.
Twenty-four are the half-months of the year;
half-month by half-month the year is reached;
thus they turn around the year.
There are five six-day periods;
they amount to thirty days.
Ten are the fingers of the hand, ten of the foot, ten are the vital breaths: that makes thirty.
The virāj has thirty syllables; man is connected with the virāj;
thus they turn around man.
"The year is unestablished," they indeed say, "broader than an established place."
This much is the brāhmaṇa of the year as of a month;
month by month they go establishing themselves, who know thus.

33.8.m:
Now these Parasāmans:
Of these Parasāmans, that which is the first is the last; that which is the last is the first; that which is in the middle remains in the middle.
Thereby, by whatever path these go, by that they return from yonder,
for the continuity, for the recognition of these worlds.
After the Viśvajit they undertake descending stomas.
Concerning this they say: "They lose what has been conquered, they undertake descending stomas that are already accomplished, they turn back from the heavenly world, and they stretch out the sacrifice facing backward."
They go in that way for five months, and of the sixth month three abhiplavas, and the two days: the Go and the Āyus.
They undertake the pṛṣṭhas: "The pṛṣṭhas are indeed the yield of the sacrifice; let us milk the sacrifice, both cattle and immortality, and let us stretch out the sacrifice facing forward."
The seasons are indeed the pṛṣṭhas.
Thereby they go establishing themselves in the seasons.
The heavenly world indeed is the pṛṣṭhas.
Thereby they go establishing themselves in the heavenly world.
Divine dominion indeed is the stomas of the Āyus.
Thereby they go ascending to divine dominion.
They undertake the chandomas.
The chandomas are indeed cattle.
They obtain cattle.
They undertake the tenth day, generative, belonging to Prajāpati, a foundation.
They obtain generation, offspring, [and] a foundation.
They undertake the Mahāvrata day.
Concerning this they say: "This day, which is the Mahāvrata day, is the solstice; it is to be undertaken at the solstice."
Then they say: "This day, which is the Mahāvrata day, belongs to Prajāpati; it is to be undertaken in the latter half of the year.
He who is born after six months, or who is born after seven months, perishes; by him one enjoys no benefit.
In a year, indeed, semen that has been deposited is brought forth.
So, when they undertake the Mahāvrata day in the latter half of the year,
they bring forth in a year this semen that has been deposited.
According to the seasons they arrange the seasons;
in proper sequence they bring forth offspring.
They undertake the twenty-fivefold stoma, the stoma of man.
Ten are the fingers of the hand, ten of the feet, two arms, two thighs, the trunk is the twenty-fifth.
When they undertake this stoma,
from their own stoma the session performers bring forth their own self,
for prosperity.
Concerning this they say: "This day which is the Mahāvrata belongs to Prajāpati; on it all cups should be drawn, for Prajāpati has pervaded, as it were, everything."
One cup should be drawn,
for Prajāpati is one.
Three should be drawn;
three are these worlds;
in these worlds they prosper.
Five should be drawn;
fivefold is the sacrifice;
they obtain the sacrifice.
Seven should be drawn;
seven are the domestic animals;
they obtain them.
Nine should be drawn;
nine are the breaths;
they establish themselves in the breaths.
Fifteen should be drawn;
fifteen are the nights of a half-month; by half-months the year is obtained;
by half-months they obtain the year.
Seventeen should be drawn;
Prajāpati is seventeenfold;
they obtain Prajāpati.
Twenty-one should be drawn;
yonder sun is twenty-first;
they obtain yonder sun.
Twenty-seven should be drawn;
thrice-nine are these worlds;
in these worlds they prosper.
Thirty-three should be drawn;
thirty-three are the deities;
they establish themselves in the deities.
Thus ends the thirty-third sthānaka, named Sattrāṇi, in the Orimikā in the Caraka recension of the glorious Kaṭha Yajurveda.

34.5.m:
Having mounted a chair, the Udgātṛ sings the laud with the Mahāvrata;
having mounted a swing, the Hotṛ recites the Mahaduktha in response.
Standing upon a standing-board, the two Adhvaryus utter the response;
the others sit on grass cushions.
The intermediate space is indeed food;
from yonder indeed it is granted;
in this [earth] it is brought forth;
therefore it is born for the intermediate space,
in that they perform [the rite] with the Mahāvrata seated in the intermediate space,
for the attainment of food and proper nourishment.
Moreover, in the presence of the gods, they conquer the high assembly;
they proceed stepping onto the heavenly world.
There are a praiser and a blamer.
The one praises the session performers, the other blames; he who praises,
whatever of theirs is well-lauded and well-recited, that he praises;
then he who blames, whatever of theirs is ill-lauded and ill-recited, that he strikes away.
A Śūdra and an Ārya pull at a hide.
The gods and the Asuras indeed contended for the sun;
the gods won it.
He causes the Āryan class to win;
he causes his own self to win.
The Ārya should be within the altar ground, the Śūdra outside the altar ground.
The hide should be white and round,
the form of the sun.
Drums sound in all the corners;
the speech that is in the quarters, that they secure thereby.
There is an earth-drum;
the speech that is in this [earth], that they secure thereby.
Lutes sound;
the speech that is in cattle, that they secure thereby.
Reed-lutes sound;
the speech that is in plants, that they secure thereby.
Flutes of reed sound;
the speech that is in trees, that they secure thereby.
The harp has a hundred strings;
man has a life-span of a hundred years, has a hundredfold virility;
he secures life-span and virility indeed.
A student of the Veda and a courtesan revile each other;
for in the world all voices sound.
They enact a copulation;
indeed, as those bringing forth offspring, these [performers] sit for a year's session;
their generative capacity is covered over by the year itself;
in that they enact a copulation,
[it is] for the attainment of the generative capacity of the year itself.
Women bearing water-jars approach,
for success.
Singing "idam madhuram," clad in armor, they go around;
they magnify the Mahāvrata itself,
and also for the possession of Indra.
The splendor of the sacred power is of one kind, that of the ruling power of another;
in that he is consecrated on a black antelope skin, that is the splendor of the sacred power;
in that, clad in armor, he has a strung bow, that is the splendor of the ruling power;
so both take place,
for the securing of both splendors.

34.6.m:
Prajāpati indeed measured out the Twelve-day rite from his own self.
Prajāpati is the year.
He took three nights from each of four months;
that became the consecration.
He took three nights from each of the next four [months];
those became the Upasads.
He took three nights from each of the next four [months];
that became the pressing.
Therefore they say: "As great as the year is, so great is the Twelve-day rite,"
for it was fashioned from the entire year.
He created the first day, and the Ehīḍa sāman, this world, fire, light;
and he created the second day, and the Ūrdhveḍa sāman, the world of the intermediate space, wind, light;
and he created the third day, and the Aiḍa sāman with iḍās, yonder world, the sun, light;
and he created the fourth day, and the Paristubdheḍa sāman, and the world in which brilliance is the light;
and he created the fifth day, and the Ādhyardheḍa sāman, and the world in which truth is the light;
and he created the sixth day, and the Hoiḍa sāman, and the world in which the sacred power is the light;
and he created the seventh day, and the Punarnitunnā iḍā, and the world in which austerity is the light;
and he created the eighth day, and the Viṣvagaiḍa sāman, and the world in which cosmic order is the light;
and he created the ninth day, and the Hoiḍa sāman, and the world in which the sacred power is the light;
and he created the tenth day, and the Dvīḍa sāman, and the world in which immortality is the light.
The in-breath is the introductory [day], the out-breath the concluding [day];
therefore, as much as is done on the introductory [day], so much is done on the concluding [day];
for as great as the in-breath is, so great is the out-breath.
These worlds, these sāmans, these lights, these splendors he secures, who knowing thus sacrifices with the Twelve-day rite.

34.11.m:
Fourfold indeed are those sets of three [nights].
By those that are the first, he purifies himself;
by those that are the second, he becomes pure, fit for sacrifice;
by those that are the third, he cleanses his limbs;
then by those that are the fourth, whatever stain he has within or without, that he shakes off.
Man indeed they eat, who officiate at the Twelve-day rite.
He who eats [the portion] of the animal sacrifice, eats meat;
he who [drinks] the whey, [drinks] blood;
he who [eats] the parched grains, [eats] bones;
he who [eats] the clarified butter, [eats] marrow;
he who [eats] the roasted grains, [eats] hair;
he who [eats] the sacrificial cake, [eats] the brain;
he who consumes the King [Soma], [consumes] sweat;
he who [eats] the groats, [eats] the feces.
He indeed eats the sacrificial session-gift who speaks untruth.
His sacrificial session-gift becomes made savory, his sacrificial session-gift is not devoured, who knows thus.
